## Claim
Text: "Donald Trump, when he thinks of climate change, he says hoax. Well, guess what? Speaking of hoaxes, remember what he said about these increasing violent hurricanes and the frequency? He actually said, maybe we should detonate a nuclear bomb over the Atlantic. By the way, the same stable genius who said the biggest problem we had in the Revolutionary War is we didn’t have enough airports."
Claim author: Joe Biden
Claim date: October 30, 2020

## Initial Q&A
### Did Donald Trump ever publicly describe climate change as a "hoax" in any speech, tweet, or interview?
Answer: Yes, Donald Trump publicly described climate change as a "hoax" in a November 2012 tweet, in which he stated, "The concept of global warming was created by and for the Chinese in order to make U.S. manufacturing non-competitive." The article also notes that "in years since has continued to call global warming a hoax."

Source URL: https://www.pbs.org/newshour/politics/trump-says-climate-change-not-a-hoax-but-not-sure-of-its-source

### What is the exact date and source (e.g., speech transcript, video, tweet) of Donald Trump's statement about detonating a nuclear bomb over the Atlantic Ocean to stop hurricanes?
Answer: The search result does not provide the exact date of Trump's statement itself, but it reports that Axios published the story on Sunday, August 25, 2019, citing sources who heard Trump ask "Why don't we nuke them?" during a hurricane briefing at the White House. The article also references a 2017 NSC memo describing a similar conversation. Thus, the primary source is the Axios report from August 25, 2019, though the specific date of the briefing is not given.

Source URL: https://www.commondreams.org/news/2019/08/26/refusing-take-real-climate-action-trump-reportedly-suggests-using-nukes-stop

### Did Donald Trump ever claim that the biggest problem during the American Revolutionary War was a lack of airports?
Answer: No, Donald Trump did not claim that the biggest problem during the American Revolutionary War was a lack of airports. The search result documents that in a July 4 speech, Trump misread a teleprompter line—originally about "manned the ramparts"—and said "took over the airports," but analysts and commentators agree this was a reading error, not an assertion that airports existed or were lacking in 1775. As one analysis notes, "there's no indication he believes there were airports in 1775." The claim attributed to Trump by Joe Biden is therefore inaccurate.

Source URL: https://languagelog.ldc.upenn.edu/nll/?p=43516

### In what specific context did Donald Trump suggest using a nuclear bomb to affect hurricanes, and was the suggestion presented as a serious policy proposal?
Answer: Based on the provided search result, Donald Trump’s suggestion to use a nuclear bomb on hurricanes was made during private White House meetings, specifically a hurricane briefing early in his presidency, where he allegedly told National Security Council officials, “we drop a bomb inside the eye of the hurricane and it disrupts it.” However, the report indicates that those present were astonished by the remark, and Trump later publicly denied the story, calling it “FAKE NEWS,” and the suggestion was not presented as a serious policy proposal but rather a private musing that was never adopted or formally proposed.

Source URL: https://www.newsweek.com/fact-check-did-donald-trump-suggest-nuking-hurricanes-1535171

### Has Donald Trump made any statements referring to climate change as a "Chinese hoax" or similar phrasing, and if so, when and where?
Answer: Yes, Donald Trump made a statement referring to climate change as a "Chinese hoax" in a tweet from 2012. The tweet, posted less than a week after Hurricane Sandy, read: "The concept of global warming was created by and for the Chinese in order to make U.S. manufacturing non-competitive." This is documented in a New Yorker article on Trump's climate change denial.

Source URL: https://www.newyorker.com/news/news-desk/trump-and-the-truth-climate-change-denial

### Is there any recorded evidence (audio, video, or written transcript) of Donald Trump discussing the Revolutionary War and airports?
Answer: Yes, there is recorded evidence. A written transcript of Donald Trump’s July 4, 2019, speech, documented by Snopes, includes his statement: “Our army manned the air, it rammed the ramparts, it took over the airports…” This speech was also captured on video and widely reported.

Source URL: https://www.snopes.com/fact-check/trump-revolutionary-war-airports/

### What was the exact wording used by Donald Trump regarding violent hurricanes and nuclear weapons, as reported by credible news organizations?
Answer: According to a report from Axios cited in the provided source, Donald Trump asked during a hurricane briefing, "Why don't we nuke them?" and elaborated: "They start forming off the coast of Africa, as they're moving across the Atlantic, we drop a bomb inside the eye of the hurricane and it disrupts it. Why can't we do that?" This represents the exact wording reported by that credible news organization.

Source URL: https://www.commondreams.org/news/2019/08/26/refusing-take-real-climate-action-trump-reportedly-suggests-using-nukes-stop

### Did Donald Trump make the statement about airports during the Revolutionary War in a public appearance, or is it a misattributed quote from satire or a joke?
Answer: DonaldTrump made the statement about airports during the Revolutionary War in a public appearance. According to Snopes, during a July 4, 2019, speech commemorating Independence Day, Trump said, "Our army manned the air, it rammed the ramparts, it took over the airports," despite no airports existing in the 18th century. The quote is not misattributed from satire; it was his actual remark, which later inspired humorous social media posts.

Source URL: https://www.snopes.com/fact-check/trump-revolutionary-war-airports/

### What was the date of Donald Trump's comment about climate change being a hoax, and was it made in his capacity as a candidate, president, or private citizen?
Answer: Donald Trump's comment about climate change being a "hoax" was made on December 6, 2013, when he tweeted "Global warming is a total, and very expensive, hoax!" This was before he declared his candidacy for president, so it was made as a private citizen. He also repeated similar language on January 29, 2014, tweeting "Global warming is an expensive hoax!"

Source URL: https://www.vox.com/policy-and-politics/2017/6/1/15726472/trump-tweets-global-warming-paris-climate-agreement

### Have any fact-checking organizations (e.g., PolitiFact, FactCheck.org, Snopes) investigated Biden's claim regarding Trump's statement on nuclear bombs and hurricanes, and what was their conclusion?
Answer: Yes, Snopes investigated Biden's claim regarding Trump's suggestion to use nuclear bombs on hurricanes. Their conclusion was "Unproven," as they could not independently verify the anonymous sources cited in the Axios report.

Source URL: https://www.snopes.com/fact-check/trump-nuke-hurricanes/

## Final Judgement
The key insights from the fact-check are as follows: Donald Trump did call climate change a "hoax" in tweets (2012–2014), supporting the first part of Biden's claim. Regarding the suggestion to nuke hurricanes, Axios reported that Trump asked about it during a White House briefing, but Snopes rated the story "Unproven" due to reliance on anonymous sources, and Trump denied it—so this part is not definitively confirmed. For the Revolutionary War airports comment, Trump actually said "took over the airports" in a July 4, 2019, speech, which was a teleprompter error; he did not say the "biggest problem" was a lack of airports, making Biden's paraphrase inaccurate. Thus, the claim contains a clear falsehood (the airports statement) and an unverified element (the nuke suggestion), while the hoax part is true.

The evidence explicitly refutes the third part of the claim, as Trump's actual words do not match Biden's description. This is a substantial part of the overall claim, and the refutation is clear. Therefore, the claim is not supported overall.

`refuted`

### Verdict: REFUTED

### Justification
The fact-check confirms that Donald Trump did call climate change a "hoax" in tweets from 2012–2014 ([Vox](https://www.vox.com/policy-and-politics/2017/6/1/15726472/trump-tweets-global-warming-paris-climate-agreement)), supporting that part of Biden's claim. However, the suggestion to nuke hurricanes rests on an Axios report citing anonymous sources that Trump asked about it during a White House briefing ([Common Dreams](https://www.commondreams.org/news/2019/08/26/refusing-take-real-climate-action-trump-reportedly-suggests-using-nukes-stop)), but Snopes rated the story "Unproven" due to the lack of verifiable evidence and Trump's denial ([Snopes](https://www.snopes.com/fact-check/trump-nuke-hurricanes/)). Most critically, Biden's paraphrase that Trump said the "biggest problem" in the Revolutionary War was a lack of airports is inaccurate: Trump actually said "took over the airports" in a July 4, 2019, speech, which analysts agree was a teleprompter error, not a claim about missing airports ([Snopes](https://www.snopes.com/fact-check/trump-revolutionary-war-airports/), [Language Log](https://languagelog.ldc.upenn.edu/nll/?p=43516)). Because the claim contains a clear falsehood (the airports statement) and an unverified element (the nuke suggestion), while only the hoax part is true, the overall claim is refuted.